# Responsive mobile-first development

All UI work in this project must be built and tested **mobile-first**, starting from the smallest supported screen width.

## Required responsive workflow

- Start design and layout decisions at **320px** first
- Then scale upward progressively
- Do not design desktop-first and then compress downward
- Every section and component must remain usable, readable, and visually balanced across all screen sizes
- Responsiveness must work not only at breakpoint widths, but also **smoothly between** them

## Primary widths to check

| Width | Role |
|-------|------|
| 320px | Smallest mobile |
| 375px | Mobile medium |
| 425px | Mobile large |
| 768px | Tablet |
| 1024px | Laptop |
| 1440px | Large laptop / desktop |
| 2560px | 4K / very large screen |

## Responsive expectations

- Layout must adapt cleanly at each breakpoint
- Spacing, typography, image scaling, card sizing, and alignment must be adjusted intentionally
- Avoid overflow, cut-off text, cramped spacing, giant empty gaps, and broken grid alignment
- Avoid elements that only look correct at one exact width
- Make sure sections scale fluidly across intermediate widths, not just the listed breakpoints
- Preserve hierarchy and premium visual quality at every screen size

## Mobile-first rules

- Prioritize smallest-screen usability first
- Use stacking layouts, spacing reduction, and typography scaling appropriately
- Ensure buttons remain tappable and readable on small screens
- Avoid hover-dependent interactions as the only way to access important content on mobile
- Make navigation, cards, forms, and animations work on touch devices

## Large-screen rules

- Prevent layouts from becoming too stretched, too sparse, or visually disconnected on large screens
- Control max widths where needed
- Maintain strong composition and balanced spacing on 1440px and 2560px screens

## When editing any section

- Consider how it behaves at **320px** before making desktop refinements
- Verify the section across all major breakpoints
- Fix responsive issues with the smallest effective change
- Do not introduce styling that breaks existing sections at other screen sizes
